4. Source Code Organization & Implementation Details

4.1 Layer 1 – Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL)

Purpose: Isolate higher layers from CPU and peripheral specifics.

Implementation Strategy:

· Written in C11 with #ifdef for platform differentiation.
· Each BSP provides:
  · hal_timer_init(), hal_timer_usleep()
  · hal_irq_register(), hal_irq_enable()
  · hal_spi_transfer(), hal_i2c_read(), etc.
· A device tree (Flattened Image Tree) is used on ARM/RISC‑V; on x86, ACPI tables are abstracted.

Directory Layout:

```
layer1_hal/
├── include/hal.h          # Public API (stable)
├── src/
│   ├── hal_common.c       # Cross‑platform implementations
│   ├── platform/
│   │   ├── arm/           # ARMv8‑A BSP
│   │   ├── riscv/         # RISC‑V 64‑bit BSP
│   │   └── x86/           # x86‑64 BSP (for ground development)
│   └── bsp_loader.c       # Selects platform at compile time
├── test/                  # Unit tests using CMocka
└── BUILD.bazel
```

Code Example – HAL timer:

```c
// hal.h
uint64_t hal_time_us(void);
void hal_delay_us(uint32_t us);

// hal_common.c (weak defaults)
__attribute__((weak)) uint64_t hal_time_us(void) {
    // fallback – should be overridden by BSP
    return 0;
}

// platform/arm/arm_timer.c
uint64_t hal_time_us(void) {
    return read_arm_generic_timer() / timer_freq_mhz;
}
```

Verification: Every BSP passes the HAL conformance test suite on real hardware (Raspberry Pi, SiFive HiFive, x86 workstation) and in QEMU.

