5. Inter‑Layer Communication Implementation

5.1 Interface Definition and Code Generation

All inter‑layer APIs are defined in Protocol Buffers v3, stored in /common/proto/.
Example – triad.proto:

```protobuf
message AuditRequest {
    string action_id = 1;
    string layer = 2;
    map<string, Value> params = 3;
    WorldModel context = 4;
}
message AuditResponse {
    enum Verdict { APPROVED = 0; REJECTED = 1; HUMAN = 2; }
    Verdict verdict = 1;
    string explanation = 2;
}
service Triad {
    rpc Audit(AuditRequest) returns (AuditResponse);
}
```

Code Generation:

· Bazel rule proto_library → cc_grpc_library, py_proto_library, etc.
· Stubs are compiled into each service and client.

5.2 Transport Protocols

Interface Type Protocol Rationale
Synchronous request‑reply gRPC over TCP (Plane B) Rich feature set, TLS, load balancing
Streaming telemetry Kafka (Plane B) Decoupled, durable, replayable
Hard real‑time TTEthernet raw frames (Plane A) Deterministic latency, bounded jitter
Shared memory POSIX shm (within same node) Zero‑copy for high‑rate data (e.g., camera frames)

5.3 Service Discovery

· Consul is used for service registration and health checking.
· Each service publishes its gRPC endpoint and TTEthernet virtual link ID.
· Clients resolve via DNS or Consul API.

